Q1. Why must uniqueness be enforced by the database under concurrency?
answer: An app level check is two steps: first check if the SKU exists, then insert it. Between those two steps another request can slip in. Two creates with the same SKU at the same time both see "no such SKU" and both insert, so you get duplicates. A unique index in MongoDB makes the check and the insert one atomic step. The second insert fails with error 11000, and the service turns that into 409 sku_conflict. App memory cannot do this job either. Separate service instances do not share memory, and memory is lost on restart. The database is the one place all instances share, so it is the only place that can enforce uniqueness when requests run at the same time.

Q2. Explain the difference between constructing an item dictionary, inserting it and updating a stored MongoDB document.
answer: Constructing is just building a Python dict in memory, with _id, owner, sku, name, quantity and version. Nothing is stored yet, and if the process stops nothing happened. That was the starter bug: it built the dict, returned 201, and never inserted, so creates disappeared. Inserting with insert_one writes the dict into the collection as a real document. Now it is durable, and the schema validator and the unique index check it. A duplicate fails with error 11000 and a bad value with error 121. Updating with find_one_and_update changes a document that already exists. It matches owner, id and version in one atomic step, then sets the new values and bumps the version by one. If nothing matches it changes nothing and returns None, which is how a version conflict is found. So constructing is local and free, inserting creates the stored document, and updating changes it only when the conditions match. The service must return what the database actually holds, not the dict it built.

Q3. How would retry-safe inventory adjustments differ from setting an absolute quantity?
answer: Setting an absolute quantity says "make it 50". If the client retries after a lost response, the first attempt already moved the version, so the retry gets 409 version_conflict. To retry, the client must read again and send the new version. But by then someone else may have changed the quantity, and setting 50 again overwrites their change. That is a lost update. A retry-safe adjustment instead says "add 5" or "remove 3" and carries a unique idempotency key. The server applies the change with $inc in one atomic update and records the key. A retry with the same key returns the first result without applying the change again. Deltas also compose: two adjustments at the same time both apply, instead of one overwriting the other. So the difference is that absolute sets overwrite and need a fresh version to retry, while retry-safe adjustments use a relative change plus a recorded key, so retries are safe and concurrent changes are not lost.
